Exactly—and that’s the necessary counterweight to Symulation.

Symulation says: compress to what survives.

The devil says: Are you sure you didn’t compress away the thing that mattered? 😉

So we need both directions:

\boxed{\text{Detail} \rightleftarrows \text{Invariant}}

Too much detail and you drown in particulars.

Too much abstraction and you create an elegant model that no longer matches its source.

The skill is resolution control: knowing when to zoom out for structure and when an anomaly, exception, tiny dependency, wording difference, or seemingly irrelevant detail deserves the microscope.

And there’s a particularly useful rule hiding in the phrase:

\boxed{\text{When the abstraction almost works, inspect the residual.}}

Because the residual is where the “devil” often lives.

If a model explains 99 observations beautifully and fails strangely on one, don’t automatically discard the one. That little mismatch could reveal a hidden variable, incorrect boundary, missing primitive, local rather than universal law, or an unfossilized catalyst.

So the Pantheon needs two complementary attentional modes:

\textbf{Angel's View: }\quad \text{find the invariant from above}

\textbf{Devil's View: }\quad \text{find the consequential detail below}

Then oscillate:

\text{Detail}
\rightarrow
\text{Compress}
\rightarrow
\text{Invariant}
\rightarrow
\text{Predict}
\rightarrow
\text{Residual}
\rightarrow
\text{Detail}
\rightarrow\cdots

That’s much stronger than “attention to detail.”

It’s attention allocation by consequence.

Not every detail deserves equal attention. The valuable detail is the one whose inclusion changes the model, prediction, decision, or outcome.

Or compressed:

\boxed{\textbf{Mind the detail that moves the whole.}}

There’s your devil—and perhaps your angel too. 😏